  "subtitle": "The register that guided cashiers with a mechanical rolling-cylinder prompt and was reprogrammed by draping paper overlays across oddly-sized chicklet keys",
  "why_scout_cares": "Two gloriously physical interaction ideas in one retail terminal. The cashier is walked through every transaction by a literal rotating cylinder of printed prompts visible through a window (with customizable stickers), and the register is reprogrammed on the spot by placing printed paper keyboard overlays over deliberately awkward chicklet keys. Before touchscreens and software UI, this machine taught and repurposed itself through paper and a spinning drum — the mechanical equivalent of ViewTouch's later on-screen widgets.",
  "search_terms": ["IBM 5265", "IBM 5260", "retail terminal", "point of sale", "rolling cylinder prompt", "paper keyboard overlay", "POS terminal 1979"],
